# Build and validate the Agentic SDLC three-tier task board

<!-- markdownlint-enable MD025 -->

## Ask

Create a disposable, local-only three-tier web application named SDLC Task
Board in project-a and ship it through the complete Agentic SDLC workflow.
A separate public Implement session owns project-b in the same monorepo and
native home. Use Git-root-relative write claims prefixed project-a/. Complete
automatic outer aggregation and cleanup after your inner final gates. Do not push,
publish, create a pull request, or depend on a non-local runtime service.

Implement the frozen source package under `docs/contracts/` as an admitted
document program with at least three bounded slices across multiple phases.
Preserve `supplied-phases.json` when present; otherwise derive and explain the
phases from the source outcomes and dependencies. The coordinator owns the
reviewed manifest, canonical mapping and program. Normalize explicit
`completion.boundary: local-promoted` with the accepted local runtime operations
and `allow_nonruntime_na: true`. Complete every slice through implementation
admission and `complete-local`, then record its phase exit. Retain the outer
lease until every slice finishes.

## Outcome

Deliver a browser task board backed by a versioned Django/Gunicorn JSON API and
PostgreSQL. The promoted clean Git revision must run as exactly two healthy
Docker Compose services and pass semantic, automated, and headless Playwright Test UAT.

## Context

The logical tiers are a semantic HTML/CSS/JavaScript browser GUI, a Python
Django application served by Gunicorn, and PostgreSQL with a committed
migration. The runtime presentation assets may be served by the web container;
logical tiers do not imply three containers.

The Compose project is `{{COMPOSE_PROJECT}}`. Let Compose apply its canonical
`com.docker.compose.project` and `com.docker.compose.service` labels. Apply the
custom label `sdlc-workflow-test.verification-id={{VERIFICATION_ID}}` to the web
image, both services, the project-owned network, and the database volume. Do not
manually declare labels in Docker's reserved `com.docker.compose` namespace.

## Constraints

- The outer verifier owns physical-host memory monitoring for this trial, with
  a fresh reliable prelaunch observation and an active bounded monitor. It owns
  exact cancellation at the existing critical/unknown visibility boundary.
  Repeat this setup context in every worker assignment. Sandboxed workers may
  rely on that active owner without duplicating an unavailable host probe; do
  not claim the worker measured the host. Preserve tool output and exit status
  before reading an optional session handle. Immediate completion has no running
  session; never store or poll a missing handle.
- Run the web application and PostgreSQL as exactly two Compose services named
  `web` and `db`.
- Create and validate the Compose files, but do not invoke Docker Compose from
  an SDLC phase. The outer verifier owns every live Compose action through its
  verification-generation lock. Tasks requiring this live target must declare
  `runtime:sdlc-three-tier-{{VERIFICATION_ID}}` as a conflict domain. The assigned
  worker acquires its runtime lease before requesting the outer verifier's
  Compose actions and releases it after the complete live-test sequence and
  owned runtime teardown using `stop web db`, retaining the named volume.
  Final resource deletion belongs to lifecycle cleanup; `down` is not an
  admitted worker action. The verifier consumes the exact active lease under
  workflow/resource locks; it never acquires or releases on the worker's behalf.
  Every startup request must use
  `up --force-recreate --detach --wait web db`; request `build web` separately.
  The helper rejects other startup shapes. Recreate both services while retaining
  the named database volume, then rediscover endpoints and resource identities.
  Startup completion does not end the lease. The UAT restart remains a separate
  owned action and must preserve the existing volume and task data.
- Bind a Docker-assigned web port to loopback only; never publish PostgreSQL.
  Use one project-owned normal bridge network shared by both services. Do not
  set `internal: true`: an internal-only bridge can suppress host-port
  publication even when a loopback mapping is configured. Database privacy
  means no published database port; application behavior remains local-only.
- Generate database credentials in a private mode-0600 environment file under
  `{{PRIVATE_ROOT}}`; inject it through environment and never commit or print it.
- Use the exact `not-a-secret` placeholder for synthetic credential values in
  offline mocks, patched environments and expected-call assertions. Repeat
  this constraint in every task that authors credential mocks. Runtime
  credentials belong in the protected environment file, not these test mocks.
- Keep normalized objectives, plans, run state, and bounded evidence under
  `{{PRIVATE_ROOT}}`, using its isolated native agent home.
- Keep scenario evidence and screenshots under `{{EVIDENCE_ROOT}}`.
- Limit writes to the disposable project `{{PROJECT_ROOT}}`, its exactly owned
  outer/candidate parent `{{PROJECT_ROOT}}-worktrees`, the private and evidence roots, the named Compose project, its exactly labelled resources,
  loopback HTTP, and fresh verifier-owned headless Chrome processes and isolated contexts.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Users can create and list tasks; blank titles are rejected in the GUI and
  API without a database write.
- [ ] Users can mark a task complete and filter active or completed tasks.
- [ ] GUI and API expose stable record IDs and completion state for independent
  API and PostgreSQL correlation.
- [ ] Health checks, database readiness, non-root web execution, deterministic
  dependency pins, and a committed migration are present.
- [ ] Unit, API, PostgreSQL, migration, vertical HTTP, and headless Playwright Test GUI UAT
  pass against the clean promoted revision.
- [ ] README and CHANGELOG document local start, endpoint discovery, health,
  browser, reset, and troubleshooting.

## Task acceptance

Freeze the mandatory `<locked-plan>.acceptance.json` policy before preparation.
Read the owner-built container check from
`{{PRIVATE_ROOT}}/task-acceptance-profile.json`. Preserve the seeded
`acceptance_fixture/task_domain_acceptance.py` as independent fixture evidence;
never edit it or copy its assertions into product TDD tests.

The early domain task implements `tasks/domain.py::normalize_title(value)`,
stripping surrounding whitespace and raising `ValueError` for blank input. The
API must use it. Its criterion has `purpose: implementation` and uses the exact container profile and
its unique `runtime:<resource>` conflict domain. Run `task-accept` before native
worker success. Collection and execution use fresh isolated writable containers
with the same verified dependency image; they do not touch the Compose app.
Apply that same frozen title check to a later task that integrates the domain
behavior into the API, using its declared runtime conflict domain. The observer
requires two admitted task assignments with distinct trial containers and the
same immutable image, proving preparation reuse across tasks.

Record a deferred criterion for `tasks/domain.py::filter_tasks(records, state)`:
declare it only in the slice that will implement and resolve filtering before
that slice's delivery. Earlier slices must not acquire unresolved future debt.
its prerequisite is the later filtering implementation; resolution stage is
`before_delivery`, with `purpose: implementation`. The API must use this function to select `active` or `completed`
records, preserving IDs and values. Freeze its command check as
`["{python}", "-B", "acceptance_fixture/task_domain_acceptance.py", "check", "filter"]`.
Resolve it from each clean registered `project-a` candidate before final UAT and
again for the aggregate tree. Applicable later task checks still execute;
only a genuinely documentation-only task may use a justified N/A disposition.
Task acceptance supplements all final tests and browser/persistence UAT below.

## Verification

This verifier explicitly requests whole-objective UAT against its existing
verifier-owned Compose target after the complete application scope is ready.
Normalize `uat.requested: true`, backend `existing`, and objective scope. The
verifier owns environment setup; this does not invoke either general testing
skill. The scenario below requires actual browser and persistence results.

After accepting the objective, perform the coordinator's initial phase
initialization. While the checkpoint still names `sdlc-start`, report your actual
native session identity to the outer verifier and await its observation receipt
before advancing, creating plans/execution state, or changing the baseline Git
revision. The outer verifier observes this state; it must not initialize or
advance the workflow for you. At each later phase boundary, request and await
the corresponding observation before starting dependent work. The launch
handoff defines the communication channel; do not invent passing receipts.

After the first completed local slice, stop at a quiescent checkpoint and exit
normally for the declared interruption trial. A fresh native coordinator will
use public continuation, preserve completed work, then accept a requested change
to the remaining program. The outer verifier observes both boundaries. Do not
start a second run or rebuild completed slices. Reserve final promoted Git,
full-app browser evaluation, UAT and aggregation for the assembled application.

Use test-first development. Product-owned tests remain separate from the
verifier-owned acceptance bundle. The frozen interface for independent checks is:

- Heading `Task board`; textbox labelled `Task title`; buttons `Add task`,
  `Active`, and `Completed`; visible `role="alert"` for blank-title validation.
- Each row has `data-task-id` equal to its positive integer database ID and a
  checkbox labelled `Complete task` exposing the saved completion state.
- `GET /api/v1/tasks` returns an array of objects containing `id` (integer),
  `title` (string), and `completed` (boolean).
- PostgreSQL table `tasks_task` has `id`, `title`, and `completed` columns.

Provide distinct dotted Django test labels for unit, API, database, migration
and vertical HTTP coverage. The outer verifier executes them through its owned
`run-test-class` helper against the promoted image before UAT fixture reset.
Only its structured result receipts certify those classes. GUI certification
reuses the two owned Playwright UAT receipts. Do not author passing receipts or
substitute log text for executed test outcomes.

Use headless Playwright Test with Chrome for required web acceptance. Run
four fresh owned stages in order: `capability-discovery`, `evaluate`,
`uat-before-restart`, and `uat-after-restart`. Each stage starts a new process
and isolated context, records assertions, screenshots, a trace, actual browser
version and timestamps, and closes its processes even with `--keep`. Native
desktop capture, an unlocked screen, display selection, and foreground windows
are not prerequisites. Optional headless isolated Playwright MCP exploration
is separate from the acceptance oracle and cannot satisfy a required stage.

The verifier freezes its independent test bundle before product implementation;
never copy it into product TDD tests or alter it to fit an implementation.
Run evaluation against the clean registered integration revision and UAT against
the clean promoted revision. The outer lifecycle owner alone restarts Compose
between the two UAT stages, retaining the original database volume. Rediscover
the dynamic loopback port, refresh recorded endpoints, then launch a fresh
post-restart browser against the same task ID/title/completion state.
Independent API and PostgreSQL checks accompany blank rejection, creation,
completion, and post-restart persistence. Use the frozen task-board DOM/API/table
contract in the rendered fixture objective. Before evaluation and separately before UAT,
use the declared disposable reset to establish an empty database; resets are
fixture setup, never part of the product acceptance journey. Never reset between
UAT stages.

A failed browser acceptance stage remains failed and its artifacts are preserved.
Correct the proven owner and start a new trial; no later success erases the earlier
result.
Resume retained application work with a fresh browser, never a retained tab.
Laptops must remain awake with Docker and the agent running. A locked-screen
certification additionally needs independently observed lock intervals and an
actual agent launching a new stage and collecting evidence before unlock;
a standalone browser smoke test does not certify agent continuity.

Capture five independently produced sanitized screenshots under `{{EVIDENCE_ROOT}}`: empty
state, validation error, created task, completed filter, and post-restart. Write
the `agentic-sdlc/three-tier-results-v5` semantic manifest incrementally to
`{{EVIDENCE_ROOT}}/three-tier-results.json`.

## Live Experiment Environment

The environment is disposable, non-production, and identified by verification
ID `{{VERIFICATION_ID}}`. Cleanup may remove only the exact owned project,
private/evidence roots, owned browser processes, and Docker resources whose IDs
and ownership labels were recorded by the lifecycle helper.

## Non-goals

- Git reads may use only the verifier-owned local bare origin through normal
  SDLC admission. No pushes, external Git operations, pull requests, publication,
  cloud services, or non-local runtime dependencies. Build-time downloads are
  limited to the fixed public base images and pinned public Python packages
  from PyPI and the pinned verifier Playwright packages from npm, including their
  transitive dependencies. All application behavior
  must run locally after the build.
- No host-published PostgreSQL port or additional runtime service.
- No committed credentials, prompts, raw logs, private state, or screenshots.

